answer:
This can be surprisingly tricky—administrators who 'fall off the radar' aren't always on your active user list, and could be lurking in disabled accounts or local groups. Here's what I'd do:

1. Open an elevated PowerShell window (Run as Administrator).
2. Check all local user accounts and their group memberships:
   ```powershell
   Get-LocalUser | ForEach-Object {
       $user = $_
       $groups = (Get-LocalGroupMember -Group 'Administrators' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object {$_.Name -eq $user.Name})
       if ($groups) {
           [PSCustomObject]@{
               UserName = $user.Name
               Enabled = $user.Enabled
               'Member of Administrators' = $true
           }
       }
   }
   ```
3. Audit disabled accounts — sometimes admins disable but don't delete:
   ```powershell
   Get-LocalUser | Where-Object { -not $_.Enabled }
   ```
4. Look in the local Security Policy for any accounts granted 'Administrator' rights through special privileges.
5. Don't forget to check domain groups if this machine is domain-joined—domain admins will have admin rights locally.

Finally, cross-reference these findings with documented accounts you know to be legit. Any enabled accounts with admin rights that you don't recognize or that are disabled but were never documented are worth a deeper look. This approach won't catch every corner case (especially if someone created accounts via scripts or third-party tools), but it should surface most of the usual suspects.
